# PHASE 03 — WINDOW MANAGEMENT

## 3.1 Unified Window Manager

Implement or consolidate a single Foundry Window Manager responsible for all viewport-level floating interfaces.

The manager should coordinate existing controller behaviors rather than duplicating their event handlers.

Window registration should support:

```json
{
  "windowId": "education-controller-main",
  "appId": "education-controller",
  "parentWindowId": null,
  "displayMode": "floating",
  "position": {
    "x": 24,
    "y": 48
  },
  "size": {
    "width": 900,
    "height": 540
  },
  "state": "normal",
  "visible": true,
  "focused": false,
  "zIndex": 100,
  "resizable": true,
  "draggable": true
}
```

These dimensions are initial examples. Adapt them to the actual viewport and existing module defaults.

## 3.2 Window Lifecycle

Support:

- Register.
- Mount.
- Show.
- Hide.
- Focus.
- Blur.
- Move.
- Resize.
- Minimize.
- Restore.
- Expand.
- Dock.
- Undock.
- Destroy.

Provide controlled lifecycle methods such as:

```javascript
windowManager.register(config)
windowManager.open(windowId)
windowManager.close(windowId)
windowManager.focus(windowId)
windowManager.move(windowId, position)
windowManager.resize(windowId, size)
windowManager.minimize(windowId)
windowManager.restore(windowId)
windowManager.expand(windowId)
windowManager.getWindow(windowId)
windowManager.getWindows()
windowManager.resetLayout(windowId)
```

Adapt method names to existing conventions where appropriate.

## 3.3 Dragging

Floating interfaces should support reliable pointer-based dragging.

Requirements:

- Use pointer capture.
- Preserve the original pointer offset.
- Prevent unintended canvas movement.
- Prevent dragging when interacting with application inputs.
- Maintain usable positioning within the viewport.
- Save final coordinates after dragging ends.
- Remove temporary listeners after completion.

Do not create conflicting drag controllers.

## 3.4 Resizing

Support independent width and height resizing.

Use:

- External L-shaped corner handles.
- Minimum and maximum dimension constraints.
- Responsive internal content.
- Pointer capture.
- Stable resize calculations from the initial dimensions.
- Persistent final dimensions.

Resize handles must not obstruct Help, Tips, navigation, or other interactive controls.

Internal panel dividers must remain independent of outer window resizing.

## 3.5 Minimize and Restore

For the Education Controller:

- Minimize into the original vertical navigation rail.
- Keep the white and blue controls visible.
- Preserve navigation icons.
- Preserve clock and status information.
- Hide associated companion panels without destroying their state.
- Restore the previous dimensions when reopened.

Other future applications may use different minimized representations.

## 3.6 Focus and Layer Management

Implement consistent window layering.

Requirements:

- Clicking a window brings it forward.
- Active windows receive appropriate focus.
- Dialogs appear above application windows.
- Context menus appear above their associated interfaces.
- Resize handles remain accessible.
- Hidden windows must not intercept pointer events.
- Floating panels must not block the entire canvas.

Only visible window surfaces should intercept canvas interactions.

## 3.7 Companion Window Relationships

Support parent-child window relationships.

```text
Education Controller
├── Choice Board
├── Essay to Slides
├── Countdown
├── Finals Study Guide
├── Digital Flashcards
├── Student Command Center
├── Lesson Planner
└── Profile and Session Timer
```

Child windows should retain their own dimensions, placement, visibility, and application state.

When the parent is minimized, hide associated children.

When the parent is restored, restore the appropriate previously visible children.

Avoid creating duplicate copies of the same application.

## 3.8 Window Persistence

Save window preferences locally using the existing persistence system where possible.

Persist:

- Position.
- Width.
- Height.
- Minimized state.
- Previous expanded dimensions.
- Internal divider positions.
- Docking preferences.
- Open application IDs.

Validate saved values when restoring layouts.

Handle viewport changes and invalid saved positions without placing windows permanently off-screen.

